\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented in the module \code{rlvm-driver}. Namespace ordering of units and separate heavy-lane threads were designed and are not built. Revised on 2026-11-25 for the change of the default protocol (\commit{a8dc6dd}): the driver no longer re-checks a rejection of the primary engine unless asked, see [the decision](dec-PUU0); the lanes and the invariants below say what that changes.}

\p{The driver runs one check of one export, from a store that is loaded or still loading to a verdict. It owns the threads, the scheduler, the life cycle of sessions, the re-check protocol, the verdict, the memory account and the statistics. It never sees an engine class (engines arrive as factories behind the facade of [the architecture](design-architecture)) and it never ends the process. How a unit's outcome becomes the verdict of the file is in [the verdict protocol](design-verdict-protocol).}

\subtree[design-driver-threads]{
\title{Threads and frontiers}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The loader} is the thread that called the driver. In a pipelined run it publishes finished units while the others check; this moves the \em{loaded frontier}. See [the export loader](design-export-loader).}
\li{\strong{The environment thread} runs the environment lane: it admits inductive blocks and the quotient records in file order with [the reference engine](design-reference-engine), and announces every unit below what it has admitted. This is the \em{environment frontier}: units below it may be handed out. See [admission](design-inductive-admission).}
\li{\strong{W workers} claim announced units and check definitions, theorems, axioms and opaques with the primary engine in a warm session. A worker has three lanes: the warm lane, a strict lane and a reference lane for re-checks. In a plain run since 2026-11-25 the reference lane serves only a unit the machine declines; the re-check of a rejection, by the strict lane and then the reference lane, runs with \code{\verb~|-X verify=reference~} and where the reference engine is the primary engine or the oracle. Since 2026-11-25 a unit that repeats an accepted unit word for word is admitted and not checked, see [repeats](design-driver-repeats).}
\li{\strong{The first-failure mark} is the smallest unit with a recorded rejection or decline. Units above it are no longer handed out, and running ones abandon at their next poll.}
}

\p{Nobody polls: the environment thread parks on the loaded frontier and is woken by a publication, workers park in the scheduler. With one worker in batch mode there is no environment thread; the worker advances the environment pass itself, in strict file order.}
}

\subtree[design-driver-scheduler]{
\title{The scheduler: slices}

\p{A worker without work takes the next \em{slice} of the announced units for itself: its share of what is ready, at least one chunk and at most 256 units. It takes chunks of 32 units from the front of its slice. When nothing is left to slice, it steals the back half of the largest remaining run. One lock is taken once per claim.}

\p{The first scheme cut every batch of ready units into one contiguous run per worker, as the reconciliation of the design review had asked. A worker that fell behind then kept receiving its share of every later batch, nobody else touched its backlog, and a heavy unit in it started late; its end was the end of the run. With slices the workers move through the file together, lowest units first. Measured on Std, check-only passes with 8 workers: 2.15 to 2.18 s before, 1.58 to 1.82 s with slices; on Mathlib no difference, steals fall from 286 to 3 (machine with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, native image, quiet-core windows). A ten-round comparison showed that slices and a split bounded to the environment batch are equal within the noise; what costs is cutting everything that is ready into W contiguous parts.}

\p{\strong{Slow units.} A unit that has run for more than 20 ms makes its worker give back the unstarted rest of its claim while the unit is still running. On reachability four units carry 93 \% of the steps and lie inside one or two claims; before this rule the other workers idled behind them. The time only decides who checks a unit, never a verdict.}
}

\subtree[design-driver-pipeline]{
\title{Pipelined loading and the parking rule}

\p{In a pipelined run the workers check what the loader has published so far. Three rules make such a run decide exactly what a batch run decides.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{"Not loaded yet" is not a verdict.} Nothing but an accept is recorded for a unit that started before the end of the load. Any other outcome is withheld, and the unit's protocol runs once more when the file is complete, because a rejection may rest on a name that a later record still provides.}
\li{\strong{The environment pass is kept} over the end of the load and its lane is reset, so that admission in dependency order can read the whole file.}
\li{\strong{The parking rule.} While the file is loading, the environment frontier does not move past a unit that may depend on the rest of the file. The driver sees that from the store's summaries without decoding anything: a root whose \code{depMax} lies beyond the unit's own rank, or a proof block with an unresolved reference. Workers go on with everything below the parked unit. Output of the exporter never parks: 0 parked units on Init, Std, cedar and Mathlib.}
}

\p{A large file is published in larger pieces: the hand-off interval of 4,096 units and 32 MiB is multiplied by the input's size in GiB, at most by 4. The reason was the price of a publication for the workers: until 2026-11-25 each publication made every worker rebuild its level overlay as a private copy of the level store, about 110 MB of garbage and 45 ms of worker CPU on Mathlib. With the scaled interval Mathlib is published 52 times instead of 205, and the pipelined run with 8 workers went from 34.25 and 35.00 s to 31.71 and 31.90 s, two interleaved rounds on the machine of that day. Pipelined loading is the launcher's default since 2026-11-24 when two or more workers run on an input of at least 8 MiB.}

\p{\strong{What a publication costs since 2026-11-25.} The workers read the frozen level store of a publication in place, see [frozen base and overlays](design-universe-levels-overlays): a lane that is handed a unit beyond its snapshot is reset as before, and the reset moves its level view to the newest snapshot with a few reference assignments. Nothing is allocated and nothing is copied; the loader still copies its open level store once per publication to go on in. The scaled interval was kept. Measured on the executable with the shared base, 8 workers, two interleaved rounds: Mathlib in 205 pieces 15.00 s and in 52 pieces 15.05 s, cslib 5.26 and 5.22 s, the same worker CPU and page faults, so small pieces no longer cost the workers anything; they are no gain either, because the workers are not waiting for the loader on these corpora and the loader pays for each piece with its own copy (1.43 s of kernel time instead of 1.10 s on Mathlib). See [the report](rep-OPT-C), section 5.3, and [the changelog entry](log-ZMAE).}
}

\subtree[design-driver-repeats]{
\title{Repeats: a unit that repeats an accepted unit is admitted and not checked again}

\p{Since 2026-11-25. The exporter writes many declarations more than once under different names: auxiliary proofs, equation lemmas, matchers. A unit is a \em{repeat} if it is a definition, a theorem or an opaque and an earlier unit of the file, its \em{original}, has the same kind, the same list of universe-parameter names, the same reducibility hint and the same two records of the export as type and as value. The loader finds repeats and writes the original into a column of the declaration table, see [the export loader](design-export-loader-repeats). 5.0 to 12.4 \% of the units of the eight corpora are repeats, 59,900 of Mathlib's 721,845, and in the build before the change they carried 1.3 to 3.7 \% of the machine's ticks.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The skip.} A worker that comes to a repeat whose original is also earlier in admission order reads the original's status on the verdict board. If it is ACCEPTED, the unit goes through the lane's admission as any unit does (the environment frontier, a recorded outcome of the environment pass, the name, the universe parameters, the roots), with a session that answers accept where the engine would have been called. A status is written once, so an ACCEPTED that was read is final. A skipped unit counts as accepted and has zero steps in the unit trace.}
\li{\strong{The original has no status yet}, because it is on another worker or in another worker's claim: the repeat is checked like any unit, and nobody waits.}
\li{\strong{The wait.} If that check does not end in accept, the worker gives the rest of its claim back, resets its session and waits for the original's status. If it is ACCEPTED, the unit goes through the path of a repeat, as it would have a moment later; otherwise the first failure of the file lies at or before the original. The wait is for a smaller unit that another worker holds, so no cycle of waits exists; the worker holds no gate, and a cancelled run ends the wait. The rule exists so that no status depends on the schedule: on one kind of hostile input the machine accepts the original and rejects its copy, see [the accept of a repeat](design-verdict-protocol-repeats). The wait did not occur on any corpus (\code{driver.repeatsAwaited} is 0); a test of the driver forces it.}
\li{\strong{Never with the reference engine as primary engine}: with \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}, and so under \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~}, every declaration is checked, as the official checker checks it. In a differential run the reference engine still checks every unit, the skipped ones included.}
\li{\strong{Setting and statistics.} \code{\verb~|-X repeats=skip~} is the default and \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~} checks every unit; \code{load.repeatUnits}, \code{driver.repeatsSkipped} and \code{driver.repeatsAwaited} count the repeats of the file, the units not checked and the waits.}
}

\p{Measured for the skip alone, the build with it against the same tree without it, warm passes with 8 workers on a calm host: in the executable the geometric mean of the eight corpora goes from 1.855 to 1.829 s, -1.4 \%, with every corpus faster; on the JVM launcher -0.7 \%, the largest loss con-leche at +1.7 \%. The loader pays half a percent to 0.8 \% of a load for its table. See [the report](rep-OPT-I), section 3, [the changelog entry](log-YGJO) and [the decision of the second wave](dec-RHRE); the build that has the skip together with the other changes of that day is [the integration of the second wave](rep-INT9).}
}

\subtree[design-driver-memory]{
\title{Sessions and memory}

\p{A worker's warm session is reset when its footprint exceeds the session cap, after any outcome but accept, when the next unit's rank is not larger than the last one's, before a unit beyond the lane's snapshot of the loading file, and when a lane holds much unused capacity after a heavy unit. The cap is per engine, see [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions). The memory account adds the shared store and one slot per checking thread. Above the limit of 12 GiB only the thread that holds the most gives up; its unit is checked again holding the \em{heavy gate}, which admits one memory retry at a time. A unit is therefore declined for memory only if it does not fit into the limit by itself.}
}

\subtree[design-driver-invariants]{
\title{Invariants and what was measured}

\ul{
\li{Fail closed: the run's status is decline until the complete file was accepted.}
\li{The verdict and the deciding unit do not depend on the worker count, the chunk and slice sizes, the session cap, the byte budgets or the load mode. No decision that affects a verdict is based on time. With the skip of repeats this holds through the wait: whether the original of a repeat was decided before the repeat came up changes no status. \strong{Qualified on 2026-11-25:} this is a property of the driver for an engine whose answer for a unit does not depend on what its session checked before. The reference engine is such an engine. The machine is not on hostile input, see the last item.}
\li{The invariance suite runs every file under 9 schedules: batch with 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers, a session cap of one byte, pipelined with a publication every 1, 7 and 64 units. On 16,928 mutants, with the reference engine and with the machine as primary engine: 0 wrong, 0 schedule differences, 1,675 declined alike in every schedule, 0 declines that only a pipelined run has. These runs were made with the re-check of a rejection, which was the default then and which the suite now asks for by name.}
\li{\strong{The machine and the schedule, measured on 2026-11-25.} A warm session of the machine keeps answers from one declaration to the next, and on hand-made input an accept or a rejection can rest on one. The suite, with the machine as primary engine: on the hostile suite of 1,274 rows 12 rows have a schedule difference with the re-check (11 tolerated accepts that need the memo of an earlier declaration, and one further row), so the invariant did not hold for the machine there before the default changed; without the re-check, the default since that day, 21 rows have one, because a rejection out of a warm session is now final where a fresh strict machine used to repair it. They are the same 11, the 8 known wrong rejects that a memo of an earlier declaration causes and two rows that get the official rejection only with the history; one of them changes with the worker count alone. On the 231 published tests of the suite's list and on 16,928 mutants, without the re-check: no file with a schedule difference. So in a plain run the invariant holds on every input on which no answer of the machine depends on its session, which is every real input measured, and the hostile rows named in [the report](rep-MO-1), section 5.4, are the known exceptions. What would restore it in both directions is a session that keeps no answer of a comparison, no checked type and no full reduction across declarations; see [the insight](ins-RPOG) and [the work list](prob-HC9J).}
}

\p{Where the wall clock of 8 workers goes was profiled on Mathlib (report P7; machine with \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, session cap 4 MiB, CE native image, quiet-core windows, development host aarch64). The check phase scales 6.9 to 7.4 times from 1 to 8 workers. Of 8 workers times the check wall clock, 87 to 91 \% is spent on a CPU in a batch run; the driver's own waits (frontier, tail, gate) are 1 \%; the serial part is the collector. Eight one-worker processes over eight shards of the file show that the 8-worker pass is 5 to 8 \% above what a perfectly balanced split could reach on that machine. After the changes above: pipelined 31.7 to 31.9 s, batch 41.5 s, a check-only pass 27.9 s.}

\p{The automatic worker count is one worker per CPU and at most 8, decided by the launcher; a batch run uses at most one worker per 128 units. The multi-pass mode that measures warm wall clock is described in [the measurement method](design-measurement-method).}
}

\p{\strong{Open.} A heavy unit that starts late is still the end of the run on mid-size corpora: on Std one unit takes 0.7 s of a 1.5 s run with 8 workers. Starting large units first needs a scheduler rule that keeps the progress property, that the lowest undecided unit is always reached; the expected gain is 2.0 to 1.7 s on Std and nothing on Mathlib.}

\p{Documents: \src{rlvm-driver/README.md} (as built, deviations, every measurement), [design-architecture-document](design-architecture-document) section 6 (the design), [rep-P7](rep-P7) (the parallel-scaling profile), \src{rlvm-main/README.md} (load defaults, worker count). Source: [the pipeline session](ses-CVTE) and [the profiles](ses-JRSD).}

